sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/rule54-universality--claude-opus-5-5--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Lean-certified completeness of the Rule 54 glider catalogue up to explicit bounds" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/rule54-universality> ;
    dct:description "Both other approaches depend on knowing all gliders, and current catalogues are lists of what has been seen, not completeness theorems. Prove in Lean 4: every pattern that is spacetime-periodic on the ether, with period p ≤ P and width w ≤ W, is one of the listed gliders up to phase and translation. The method is de Bruijn-diagram enumeration. Periodic patterns of period p correspond to cycles in a finite graph whose vertices are width-2p words, so completeness reduces to enumerating cycles in finite graphs. Extract the enumeration as a certificate (cycle list plus a proof that no other cycle passes the ether boundary conditions) and check it in Lean by kernel reduction. Pick P and W so the kernel check stays feasible (P = 24 first, then extend)." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A Lean theorem 'the gliders of Rule 54 on ether with period ≤ P and width ≤ W are exactly G_1 … G_k' plus a verified collision table. This does not settle universality, but turns the search space of approaches 1 and 2 from an observation into a proved object." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The de Bruijn graphs for useful P are too large for kernel checking (they grow as 2^(2p) vertices), capping the theorem at a P too small to include the gliders Martínez et al. used for gates." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define the de Bruijn graph for period-p spacetime patterns in Lean and prove the correspondence theorem (periodic pattern ⇔ graph cycle). 2. Generate the cycle enumeration externally for each p ≤ P with ether boundary conditions. 3. Check the certificate in Lean; derive the completeness theorem for the glider list. 4. Repeat for the binary collision table: prove that outcomes for all pairs from the complete list are as tabulated." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: de-bruijn-diagrams" ;
    skos:note "Tool: cellular-automaton-simulation" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-21> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-19> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-20> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chaos.2005.05.013> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/rule54-universality> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Is Rule 54 computationally universal?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chaos.2005.05.013> dct:title "Phenomenology of glider collisions in cellular automaton Rule 54 and associated logical gates" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Martínez, Adamatzky and McIntosh 2006, catalogue to be certified" .
}

